Maybe.  However, if we look at the other gods for monks:  the lawful
God "Shan Lai Ching", whose name I have never heard and I doubt as a
misspelling of "Shan Hai Ching", a famous collection of ancient Chinese
mythology ( http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shan_Hai_Jing ), and the
neutral God "Chi Sung Zhi", the God of Rains whose name appeared in so
many stories of Taoist pursuit of immortality ( including a well-known
strategist who helped created the Han Dynasty in the 3rd century BC ).
I believe the Devteam is thinking about the Yellow Emperor, not Shi
Huang Ti.

What interests me if the definition of Chaotic, Neutral and Lawful in
AD&D.  If we look at the setting for samurai: the lawful Goddess
"Amaterasu Omikami", the Goddess who Shines the Sky, put an end to
chaos aroused by her mischievous brother, and gave birth to the first
emperor in the Japanese mythology.  the chaotic God "Susanowo", is the
younger brother of Amaterasu Omikami who made a mess of heaven and
earth just because he refused his father's assignment as the God of
Seas.  That fits my ideas of lawful and chaotic quite well as a layman
to AD&D, and I believe we can find similar pattern for all other
cultural settings. That's why I have some doubts for the setting of
monks.

IMHO, the religion settings should be

Lawful: Nu Wa, the Goddess who repaired the sky and rebuild the human
race after the War of Gods, http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N%C3%BCwa
Neutral: Pan Gu, the first immortal created the universe be tearing
apart the ancient chaos.  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pangu
Chaotic: Gong Gong, the God of Waters who was defeated by the God of
Fire in the War of Gods.  He committed suicide after the defeat by
hitting his head on a mountain that supports the sky, a disaster which
almost wiped out the human race.



